### 2.7 Collective Classification

Collective classification is a different way of revealing company hierarchy from a graph perspective. This approach uses the connection between nodes to propagate labels within the whole network. Loopy belief propagation is an example of collective classification described in detail in References [Sen et al. (2008)](https://arxiv.org/html/1906.09576v2#bib.bib28); [Kajdanowicz et al. (2016)](https://arxiv.org/html/1906.09576v2#bib.bib41). Therefore, in this paper, a simplified version of this algorithm is introduced to compare with standard classification algorithms.

The proposed collective classification method is presented as Algorithm [1](https://arxiv.org/html/1906.09576v2#alg1 "Algorithm 1 ‣ 2.7 Collective Classification ‣ 2 Materials and Methods ‣ Combining Machine Learning and Social Network Analysis to Reveal the Organizational Structures"). The first step of this algorithm is choosing a utility score and sorting all nodes according to it (line [1](https://arxiv.org/html/1906.09576v2#alg1.l1 "In Algorithm 1 ‣ 2.7 Collective Classification ‣ 2 Materials and Methods ‣ Combining Machine Learning and Social Network Analysis to Reveal the Organizational Structures")). The utility score can be one of the calculated features from the previous section. The next step is to reveal the labels for the given percentage of nodes of each class li∈Ll\_{i}\in L with the highest utility score (line [2](https://arxiv.org/html/1906.09576v2#alg1.l2 "In Algorithm 1 ‣ 2.7 Collective Classification ‣ 2 Materials and Methods ‣ Combining Machine Learning and Social Network Analysis to Reveal the Organizational Structures")). These nodes are marked as known (their labels are constant) and labeled VLV^{L} , whereas the other nodes are treated as unkown VU​KV^{UK} and unlabeled. Furthermore, the propagation of labels begins in a loop until the stop condition is met or the number of iterations exceeds the given maximum number of iterations. In one iteration, each labeled node sends a message to all of its neighbors by treating edges as undirected (line [5](https://arxiv.org/html/1906.09576v2#alg1.l5 "In Algorithm 1 ‣ 2.7 Collective Classification ‣ 2 Materials and Methods ‣ Combining Machine Learning and Social Network Analysis to Reveal the Organizational Structures")); moreover, all received labels in a given iteration are saved for each node viv\_{i} in a counter cvic\_{v\_{i}} (line [6](https://arxiv.org/html/1906.09576v2#alg1.l6 "In Algorithm 1 ‣ 2.7 Collective Classification ‣ 2 Materials and Methods ‣ Combining Machine Learning and Social Network Analysis to Reveal the Organizational Structures")). The labels update begins after all nodes sent a message to their neighbors, so the sending order does not affect the result. If the node viv\_{i} has received one label more often than others (line [13](https://arxiv.org/html/1906.09576v2#alg1.l13 "In Algorithm 1 ‣ 2.7 Collective Classification ‣ 2 Materials and Methods ‣ Combining Machine Learning and Social Network Analysis to Reveal the Organizational Structures")), this label will be assigned to it (line [14](https://arxiv.org/html/1906.09576v2#alg1.l14 "In Algorithm 1 ‣ 2.7 Collective Classification ‣ 2 Materials and Methods ‣ Combining Machine Learning and Social Network Analysis to Reveal the Organizational Structures")) and the node will be additionally treated as labeled vi∈VLv\_{i}\in V^{L} (line [15](https://arxiv.org/html/1906.09576v2#alg1.l15 "In Algorithm 1 ‣ 2.7 Collective Classification ‣ 2 Materials and Methods ‣ Combining Machine Learning and Social Network Analysis to Reveal the Organizational Structures")), otherwise for this node counter uviu\_{v\_{i}} will be increased (line [18](https://arxiv.org/html/1906.09576v2#alg1.l18 "In Algorithm 1 ‣ 2.7 Collective Classification ‣ 2 Materials and Methods ‣ Combining Machine Learning and Social Network Analysis to Reveal the Organizational Structures")). If uviu\_{v\_{i}} exceeds the maximum value (line [20](https://arxiv.org/html/1906.09576v2#alg1.l20 "In Algorithm 1 ‣ 2.7 Collective Classification ‣ 2 Materials and Methods ‣ Combining Machine Learning and Social Network Analysis to Reveal the Organizational Structures")), it will be reset (line [26](https://arxiv.org/html/1906.09576v2#alg1.l26 "In Algorithm 1 ‣ 2.7 Collective Classification ‣ 2 Materials and Methods ‣ Combining Machine Learning and Social Network Analysis to Reveal the Organizational Structures")) and the node will be assigned the label with the highest position in the company hierarchy among the labels with the highest count (lines [22](https://arxiv.org/html/1906.09576v2#alg1.l22 "In Algorithm 1 ‣ 2.7 Collective Classification ‣ 2 Materials and Methods ‣ Combining Machine Learning and Social Network Analysis to Reveal the Organizational Structures") to [24](https://arxiv.org/html/1906.09576v2#alg1.l24 "In Algorithm 1 ‣ 2.7 Collective Classification ‣ 2 Materials and Methods ‣ Combining Machine Learning and Social Network Analysis to Reveal the Organizational Structures")). At the end of iteration the stop conditions is always checked and it is determined as a difference between sets of previous and current labels, therefore if the Jaccard coefficient is bigger than the given minimum Jaccard value and all nodes have assigned label then the algorithm will end (line [29](https://arxiv.org/html/1906.09576v2#alg1.l29 "In Algorithm 1 ‣ 2.7 Collective Classification ‣ 2 Materials and Methods ‣ Combining Machine Learning and Social Network Analysis to Reveal the Organizational Structures")). Additionally, in the case of unbalanced classes, the algorithm allows defining a t​h​r​e​s​h​o​l​dthreshold. During the phase of counting how many times each label was received by the node, the result for the majority class will be divided by this threshold to prevent domination of this class. (line [11](https://arxiv.org/html/1906.09576v2#alg1.l11 "In Algorithm 1 ‣ 2.7 Collective Classification ‣ 2 Materials and Methods ‣ Combining Machine Learning and Social Network Analysis to Reveal the Organizational Structures"))

